There is one condition, and it is not optional: you must keep the original copyright notice and permission text intact in any copy or substantial portion of the code. This is not a suggestion. The MIT License explicitly requires it. Crediting the original author (Derek) in your project documentation is appreciated if you use this publicly, but the license itself only mandates retaining the notice below.

The "as is" clause at the bottom means exactly what it says. If you deploy this code and something goes wrong (your database gets corrupted, your Discord tokens get rate-limited into oblivion, your Lavalink server catches fire, or your hosting provider sends you an angry email about CPU usage), that is your problem. The authors and copyright holders are not liable for any damages, whether you are running this on a dedicated server or a Raspberry Pi taped to your router.

If you are not sure what the MIT License allows, the short version is: you can do almost anything with this code as long as you include the license text. The long version is below.

